WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.360
Like so many other Simpsons characters, Maggie&nbsp;
is fully realized and comes with her own unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.400
set of quirks. Although she can't walk more than&nbsp;
a few steps without falling and rarely speaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.320
she is one of the show's most beloved characters.&nbsp;
Here are just some of Maggie's many great moments.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.560
One of the biggest television news stories of&nbsp;
1995 parodied the biggest television news story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.080
of 1980. The sixth season of The Simpsons&nbsp;
ended on a cliffhanger: who shot Mr. Burns?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.200
And the seventh-season opener promised to&nbsp;
end months of speculation, much like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.040
"who shot J.R.?" publicity stunt pulled by the&nbsp;
primetime soap Dallas more than a decade earlier.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.800
It's assumed that Burns' loyal&nbsp;
assistant, Smithers, committed the act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.160
Before descending into a boozy stupor, Smithers&nbsp;
remembers feeling the need to stop Burns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.680
and he later finds a discharged pistol in his&nbsp;
coat pocket. Police only have two witnesses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.200
the crime — Santa's Little Helper and baby Maggie,&nbsp;
who reveal nothing in an interrogation because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.320
well, they can't talk. However, that may&nbsp;
be out of a desire to not self-incriminate.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.600
Mr. Burns emerges from a coma and identifies&nbsp;
the shooter as Maggie Simpson. What happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.040
was that the awful old guy literally tried to&nbsp;
steal candy from a baby. In this case, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.400
Maggie with a lollipop. He fought her for it, and&nbsp;
in the struggle, his gun fell out of its holster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.680
landed in Maggie's hands, and accidentally&nbsp;
went off. While recovering in his hospital bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.840
Burns insists that Maggie be arrested.&nbsp;
However, Chief Wiggum knows better…

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.960
"Yeah, right, Pops. No jury in the world's&nbsp;
gonna convict a baby. Hmmm, maybe Texas."

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.120
Each member of the Simpson family has a few&nbsp;
distinguishing characteristics. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.000
Bart is a troublemaker, Marge is totally square,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.520
and Maggie doesn't speak. That's partially a&nbsp;
function of her being a baby, but a whole lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.920
of comedy can be mined from a character&nbsp;
who's very intelligent but stays silent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.120
However, Maggie does speak from time to time,&nbsp;
and it's on such rare occasions that when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.400
does happen, it's a certified TV event, with a&nbsp;
big celebrity lending their voice to the role.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.680
The 1992 flashback episode "Lisa's First&nbsp;
Word" features Lisa as a newborn baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.960
and it ends with Homer putting Maggie to bed&nbsp;
and quipping that he hopes she never talks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.840
so that she'll never talk back to him. After&nbsp;
he leaves, Maggie delivers the magic moment…

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.760
"Daddy" (puts pacifier back in)

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:31.600
Making Maggie's first word even more impressive is&nbsp;
that it was voiced by guest star Elizabeth Taylor.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.440
Then, in the 1994 "Treehouse of Horror" segment&nbsp;
"Time and Punishment," Homer careens through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.760
different realities, landing in one where&nbsp;
Maggie kills Groundskeeper Willie with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.800
axe. And that's when she speaks again, only&nbsp;
this time in the voice of James Earl Jones...

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.400
"This is indeed a disturbing universe."

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.800
Marge finally cracks under the stress of managing&nbsp;
a household and raising three kids with no help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.640
from her husband in the 1992 episode "Homer&nbsp;
Alone," necessitating a trip to Rancho Relaxo.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.080
"Remember, you can't spell&nbsp;
'Relaxo' without 'relax’."

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.040
But Marge doesn't trust Homer to watch&nbsp;
the children for even a couple of days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.400
and she shuttles Bart and Lisa off to the sad&nbsp;
apartment of aunts Patty and Selma. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.480
Maggie is smart enough to know that's a&nbsp;
bad scene, and she refuses to leave home.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.040
Despite Marge's reservations, Maggie stays&nbsp;
with Homer. She was right to worry, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.160
because Homer and Maggie are lost without her.&nbsp;
Maggie is just a baby, after all, and she doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.760
understand why her beloved mother suddenly&nbsp;
disappeared, albeit temporarily. So after Homer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.400
puts her to bed and almost immediately trashes&nbsp;
the house with Barney, she crawls out of her crib,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.800
out through the doggy door, and into the&nbsp;
Springfield night in search of Marge.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.640
On the surface, this is a plot about a baby&nbsp;
wandering the streets in the wee hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.240
but it's not too troubling because The Simpsons&nbsp;
is a family cartoon, and Maggie is surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.560
self-sufficient. The running gag for most of&nbsp;
the episode is that Springfield is full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.160
things and people that look like Marge and get&nbsp;
Maggie's hopes up, including a shrub, a giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.200
ice cream cone sign, and a guy in a beefeater hat&nbsp;
working at a British-themed quick lube business.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.680
"Lube job while you wait?"

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:12.320
"Don't touch me."

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.160
As the youngest, least mobile, and almost&nbsp;
completely silent member of the Simpson family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.640
Maggie is rarely the center of a main plot&nbsp;
or subplot of an episode of The Simpsons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.040
a show that, despite being animated and&nbsp;
where time doesn't pass and people don't age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.200
trades in realism. However, Maggie&nbsp;
gets her rare chance to take center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.680
stage in a delightfully surreal way&nbsp;
during the 2015 episode "Puffless."

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.960
Closer in form to one of the Simpsons-themed&nbsp;
theatrical shorts, the episode's secondary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.360
plot — known as "Maggie's Extraordinary&nbsp;
Animal Adventure" — is a self-explanatory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.760
self-contained lark in which Maggie makes friends&nbsp;
with a squirrel outside of her bedroom window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.600
which is her entry into a secret world&nbsp;
of adorable critters, including an owl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.880
opossum, and Duffman's pet parrot. Maggie&nbsp;
takes charge and saves the day when local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.760
yokel Cletus traps the opossum so he can&nbsp;
cook him up and feed him to his large family.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.840
"Hey, Brandine! I caught us a possum! You&nbsp;
want to name it before we eat it or after?"

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:10.980
"During."

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.440
Thanks to Maggie, the friendly opossum&nbsp;
is freed after a full-on assault from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.760
the Springfield animal community. And it's&nbsp;
all done without anybody speaking a word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.480
which is great news for Maggie and the animals but&nbsp;
it's a serious disappointment for Cletus's kids.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.560
"Okay, well, uh, possum's gone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.720
But you got fingernails to chew&nbsp;
on. Who knows what's under them?"

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.160
The 1992 episode "Brother, Can You Spare&nbsp;
Two Dimes?" staged a reunion between Homer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:39.520
and his half-brother, Herb Powell.&nbsp;
He was left homeless and eating out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.920
of garbage cans after Homer's failed car&nbsp;
design bankrupted his automobile company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.320
So how does Maggie play into this narrative?

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.040
Well, The Simpsons was barely into its third&nbsp;
season, but it hit so big and thoroughly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.600
fans were already clamoring to find out what&nbsp;
was on the mind of the baby of the family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.040
They got their chance when Uncle Herb,&nbsp;
in an attempt to restore himself to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.840
wealthy lifestyle, invents a machine that&nbsp;
translates baby talk into plain English.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.200
He does it with the help of a&nbsp;
$2,000 investment from Homer.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.360
The almost always silent Maggie talks&nbsp;
a lot in this episode, or at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.360
she babbles like a typical baby. But&nbsp;
thanks to Herb's brilliant invention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.840
everyone can finally understand the&nbsp;
complexities of Maggie's deepest thoughts.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.040
(Maggie babbles)

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.240
"I have soiled myself. How embarrassing."

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.000
Through a series of strange events and&nbsp;
misunderstandings, a 1995 Simpsons episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.240
finds the county visiting Homer and Marge's&nbsp;
home and deciding that they're unfit parents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:39.120
The kids are immediately placed in a child&nbsp;
welfare van and driven to a foster home.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:45.280
"Yay!"

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.480
"Heydilly-ho. Welcome to your&nbsp;
new home, neglect-areenos."

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.840
While the episode is primarily a Bart and&nbsp;
Lisa adventure, focusing on the two older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.160
Simpson kids as they miss their parents and&nbsp;
struggle against the creepy vibe of Flanders'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.360
TV-restricted environment, Maggie shines in a&nbsp;
big way throughout the story. She's the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.480
government agents ultimately decide to reassign&nbsp;
the Simpson kids, as she's found wearing a sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.840
Bart originally placed on Lisa's back reading&nbsp;
"I'm a Stupid Baby." And one of the big twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.320
of the episode is that Maggie really responds well&nbsp;
to her new lifestyle, relishing in the attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.760
bestowed on her by the Flanders. At one point,&nbsp;
she even removes her pacifier so she can babble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:29.840
and call Ned "daddily-doodily." Luckily, the&nbsp;
spell is broken when she finally sees her mom.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.960
"Oh, Maggie, you're a Simpson again."

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:33.493
(burps)

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.760
1992's "A Streetcar Named Marge" finds Marge&nbsp;
starring in a community theater's musical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:44.720
version of the extremely dark Tennessee&nbsp;
Williams play A Streetcar Named Desire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:50.080
But because Homer is such an oafish lout, she has&nbsp;
to bring Maggie along to rehearsals. After Maggie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.880
disrupts an intense scene between Marge and Ned&nbsp;
Flanders, the play's director Llewellyn Sinclair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:00.160
forces Marge to drop her off at his sister's&nbsp;
daycare center, the Ayn Rand School for Tots.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.600
Inspired by the philosophies of Rand, Sinclair&nbsp;
runs a harsh school that promotes stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.200
individualism and encourages children&nbsp;
to "develop the bottle within," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:14.880
includes confiscating all pacifiers. That's&nbsp;
a big problem for pacifier-addicted Maggie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.840
who tries sucking on her thumb, crayons, blocks,&nbsp;
and anything else she can get her little hands on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:24.800
Eventually, she attempts to break into the locker&nbsp;
where the confiscated pacifiers are stored.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.840
However, she gets caught in the act and thrown&nbsp;
in "the Box" — a playpen equating solitary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:35.520
confinement. The centerpiece of the B-plot, a&nbsp;
grand parody of The Great Escape, soon commences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.000
Maggie brilliantly sneaks into the daycare&nbsp;
center's vent system and lowers herself into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.720
Ms. Sinclair's office with the string from a&nbsp;
talking Krusty the Clown doll, she then grabs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.120
the keys to the pacifier locker, and uses&nbsp;
a makeshift zipline of her own devising to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:53.840
access the forbidden treasure. Despite the odds,&nbsp;
Maggie's plan works even better than expected.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.060
"Maggie, time to go to the (screams).”

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.016
(babies with pacifiers)]

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.200
A 1997 episode of The Simpsons, "My Sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.200
My Sitter," looks into how funny it can&nbsp;
be when Maggie acts like an actual baby.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.480
At the beginning of the story,&nbsp;
Lisa proves herself to be such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.240
dependable and mature kid that she&nbsp;
becomes a successful babysitter.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:16.880
"What do you say, Homer? Can&nbsp;
Lisa babysit for my kids?"

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:17.840
"Please, please, please."

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:19.360
"Eh, I'll have to ask her."

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.960
Homer and Marge even trust her to look&nbsp;
after Bart and Maggie when they head off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.920
to the fancy grand opening of Springfield's&nbsp;
South Street Squidport shopping district.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:35.120
The idea of younger Lisa in charge enrages&nbsp;
Bart, and he sets out to make the night as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.160
bad as possible for his babysitter, refusing to&nbsp;
eat dinner and getting ice cream all over himself.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:44.800
That fake tantrum provokes a real one in a&nbsp;
stress-sensing Maggie, and Bart gives her ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:51.520
cream to calm her down. One problem — it's coffee&nbsp;
ice cream. It jacks up Maggie into a twitching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:55.440
grinning, wide-eyed caffeine monster&nbsp;
who crawls on the shower curtain rod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.080
sprays talcum powder on Lisa, and runs&nbsp;
off. After Bart knocks himself out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:05.680
Lisa has to safely get the whole crew across&nbsp;
town to Dr. Nick Riviera's sketchy clinic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.800
Since Maggie is so thoroughly and hilariously&nbsp;
agitated from the coffee, Lisa puts her in a cat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.640
carrier because she won't stop sticking&nbsp;
her fingers in passed-out Bart's face.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.680
"And as a grim finale, she intends&nbsp;
to drown that poor caged baby."

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.600
Maggie often seems to be off doing her own thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.760
as she's mentally and physically separated&nbsp;
from her older, verbal family members.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:33.840
That idea persists in the many future-set episodes&nbsp;
of The Simpsons, in which Maggie is the only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:39.280
in the family who's both successful and at peace&nbsp;
with herself. Homer seems to die an awful lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.720
Bart winds up a destitute bar musician, Lisa&nbsp;
is president but stuck in bad relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:49.120
all while Maggie is off living the life she&nbsp;
wants to live and doing so as early as age nine.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.600
In "Future-Drama," she isn't home on&nbsp;
Bart and Lisa's prom night. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:58.720
Marge shows off a moving photo of Maggie waving&nbsp;
from a beach in Alaska. She doesn't speak a word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.840
as is The Simpsons' way with Maggie. Audiences&nbsp;
also don't hear a sound from 30-something Maggie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:10.000
in "Holidays of Future Past," by which point&nbsp;
she's become a world-famous rock star. Amusingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.880
future Maggie is also the eventual mother of&nbsp;
a quiet baby who looks exactly like her mom.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.880
"So, who's the father? Eh...it doesn't matter.”

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:24.800
Disney welcomed The Simpsons to its Disney+&nbsp;
service in 2020 with a short titled "Playdate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:31.280
with Destiny." In this completely dialogue-free&nbsp;
story, Marge takes Maggie to Not Responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:36.800
Injuries Park where she meets a cute boy that&nbsp;
viewers learn is named Hudson, at least that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:42.080
the name written on his diaper. It's love at first&nbsp;
sight. As they hit the various park attractions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:47.120
Maggie and Hudson imagine they're engaged&nbsp;
in grand romantic activities — horsey spring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:51.840
riders become real horses, a drinking&nbsp;
fountain doubles as champagne for two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:55.760
and dining in Paris is, in reality,&nbsp;
sharing sand in the playground.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:00.960
It all ends during a game of peekaboo, when Maggie&nbsp;
is surprised and heartbroken to see Hudson's mom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:05.680
loading him into their car. After a Goodnight,&nbsp;
Moon-themed dream in which everything turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:10.720
into Hudson's face, Maggie preps for her next&nbsp;
playground date, only to find to her horror that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:16.640
Homer is on kid duty. Unfortunately, he takes her&nbsp;
to a skate park because they've got a taco truck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.360
The next day, Maggie doesn't take any chances and&nbsp;
wrestles control of the car away from Homer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:26.320
drives them to Not Responsible for Injuries&nbsp;
Park. It's there that she finally reunites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:31.040
with Hudson for their first kiss. Well —&nbsp;
more like a baby version of a first kiss.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:37.280
(Puckering up, then exchanging pacifiers)]

